 think this is a topic that  will be of interest to to our audience  um and  i'm curious if you could go back to when  you were a student what would you tell  yourself from this perspective and in  particular  do you have any advice for the minority  students  who are starting their careers  well i'll um i mean i can kick off um  with some of my perspective um maybe not  not in terms of um  advice but maybe just perspective um  is that you know within amazon  internships are extremely valuable for  our future leaders and um and and i  think the great thing is is that you  typically do not work  uh on um on deliverables that the  business is already signed up for but  you you have have the luxury of working  on things that are um a little bit more  think big  items uh that help us shape our thinking  of the future  um  the the the challenges is that some of  that comes with a lot of ambiguity so so  as you as you think about taking and  embarking on an internship within amazon  you know be prepared to um kind of have  a high level problem that you're trying  to solve for um  and then and then use a lot of your um  uh own intuition  and data gathering capabilities to to  kind of narrow it down  to exactly what the what the problem is  um  i'll give you an example is that uh last  year  uh we  uh wanted to see if um  simple contacts within our space you  know contacts from our customers could  be automated through natural language  processing but we really just had no  experience in building  any of the these capabilities in terms  of you know voice or chat  bots  so so we tasked our intern  with this particular problem  and this this particular intern didn't  have any tech background in this  particular space um and he he had to  kind of figure out the vagueness of this  problem and and really what what it is  that he's trying to solve for  um and he really went and solved it in  chunks you know so so he looked at what  are the things what are the sub  components of this how to prioritize it  and then choose the battle that that you  want to fight which is um just the proof  of concept chatbot  um with limited tech resources so we  really didn't have a lot of tech  resources to support that um that  particular solution but he managed to do  that he managed to bubble it down  all within  three months  and i think coming in with that bias for  action uh being comfortable with  ambiguity  uh and um asking for help when you need  it is really the key to success for any  internship  um and and then  eventually you know that that both both  your performance and your idea may may  actually result in additional funding  for the following year and something you  can drive which is in fact what has  happened with this particular example so  i think it's a great example of of being  able to come into um an area that that  maybe you don't know a lot about maybe  maybe the subject is a little bit vague  to you but really creating chunkable  components where you can actually  address one problem at a time  and then and then and then coming up  with a solution uh that's that's  plausible and feasible uh in future so  that would be uh my advice in terms of  how to handle um the the internship  now in the in the last mile we have had  the internship for the last couple of  years and i can tell you that working  with insurance have been absolutely  rewarding experience  in amazon we treat interns like new  hires  and they're up to that experience  scientists  uh they're asked to find a problem  unlike anybody else in amazon they are  you have to work partners from the  customer need  to develop an appropriate solution  in this process they want to find  the inputs and the success metrics and  they propose a solution that is back it  up with good hard data and then present  the results  in some cases we have actually  implemented some of the internal  solutions into life operations  uh  and the goal is not only to challenge  them too hard and to solve hard  real-world problems  but also  get them to a level where they want to  come back when they finish the big weeks  in fact in the last month we have  several interns that are now part of the  team  thank you and i i agree i found actually  that what impressed me the most i get  and i can speak as a reasonably new hire  at uh at amazon  uh what impressed me as  in the way that we structure internships  for our interns is that we strive for  each one of them to be a complete  project so something that a person can  own from beginning to end  that they can put on their resume as a  completed project  something that we strive actually to use  in practice  something that probably will be a new  idea so in many ways  these internship projects are really  very interesting and  we provide our interns with a lot of  support we want them to be successful we  want what they've been investigating to  be something we can continue to use in  the long term so overall i find that you  know a lot of our interns are very happy  and like we're happy about that so it  works both ways and just today actually  we had a launch announcement for a  dashboard that was completed by our  summer intern  and this intern was a second year at  university so you can  imagine that there was a lot of very new  very new technologies that he came in  with  contact with for the first time and you  know we were able to support him  and uh you know get it to the point that  something that we can actually keep in  use so i'm just very proud of how that  worked out and one uh one advice i would  give from my perspective to our interns  is to not not shy away from being a  little bit greedy like look  look for what you can take away from the  experience try to learn as much as you  can  try to meet people you know learn from  them ask them about their own experience  uh because you know all of that is  provide some enrichment and hopefully  you know some useful context in the  future and also helps you evaluate if  that if the environment is right for you  and you can make make better decisions  um you know in the future when you  choose your future workplace  and i would like to highlight something  very quickly uh here so the point of  ambiguity right that was brought earlier  it so it seems like when um especially  in the context of internships when you  know  time  is bounded and there's so much pressure  right um  layer that that you know interns will be  facing a business problem a real world  problem right and  those problems usually come with at  least a small amount of ambiguity right  and that creates a lot of anxiety but  ambiguity is really a characteristics of  of the business right and that's why you  know that's that's why companies have  you know all these teams of um  scientists right trying to resolve that  uh to to uh you know crystallize the  solutions for ambiguous problems even  defining the problem is really making  progress so as an intern right  especially in the early parts of the of  the project you will see that there's  probably a little bit uh  um  you know maybe sometimes a lot of sync  what it seems a lot of ambiguity right  but that's really a characteristics of  the of the problems that you know  businesses face  yes thank you everyone  and let's talk a little bit about what  is new and exciting  in the optimization space at amazon  and also if you want to build on the  previous question about how you can  overcome barriers to be successful if  you have any other thoughts you can add  to that  yeah i was going to say i think um  i talked a little bit about a specific  thing in myspace but if you look really  broadly at what we're doing at amazon i  mean you've probably seen one of our  thousands of prime trailers with the  kind of amazon smile on the side but you  may not have seen that there's actually  also a solar powered gps  device on on top of that trailer that  allows us to track every  um asset across the network  we know exactly where they are and we're  able to use that gps to help address  defects before they impact customers  we also once the trailers get into our  yards we pair that gps with vision  systems that recognize the license  plates  um they create an ability for us to have  a touchless check-in process where  there's no contact between kind of the  guard checking in and  the driver that that's driving that  trailer which has been a big asset  during covid  um and then behind all the scenes we  also built state-of-the-art routing uh  algorithms for aircrafts and and trucks  um they evaluate trillions of  permutations to optimize our network  in fact we were actually awarded the  informs prize in recognition for  long-standing application of science to  our logistics so i mean i think overall  like we we continue to innovate and use  kind of optimization  in order to  build a better network for our customers  um in terms of kind of overcoming  barriers none of these things were  accomplished kind of  uh easily i think everything has hit  roadblocks i talked about a project  earlier in my team that's you know now  kind of  eight months delayed um but it's about  kind of learning from that um  learning from that opportunity learning  from the mistake  uh and then going  at it from a different angle or  improving so it's not about um  kind of giving up and saying like if  like we still really value the idea and  we need a different approach to get  there um so that kind of fail fast  mentality although i'd say eight months  is probably uh not necessarily failing  quite as fast as we would have liked to  um  but we use that to continue to overcome  barriers and to um  to kind of re-approach problems  yeah and i think that's a really  important point i think even if we're  successful we're still continuing to  iterate on our ideas and make them  better so  definitely agree with that  okay i think that brings us to the end  of our conversation today so first of  all thank you  to all of our panelists for allowing us  to get a glimpse of the challenging  problems you're working on in your space  and for sharing how you encourage  diversity on your teams  and thank you  to everybody watching for attending our  conversation today  for more info  and if you'd like to learn about  opportunities at amazon you can actually  email us at  informs2021 at amazon.com  and also please visit amazon.science and  you can stay up to date on our most  recent news and announcements  and thank you all and enjoy the rest of  the conference  you
 

